       veth - Virtual Ethernet Device

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The vveetthh devices are virtual Ethernet devices.  They can act as tunnels
       between network namespaces to create a bridge to a physical network de‐
       vice  in  another namespace, but can also be used as standalone network
       devices.

       vveetthh devices are always created in interconnected pairs.  A pair can be
       created using the command:

           # ip link add <p1‐name> type veth peer name <p2‐name>

       In the above, _p_1_‐_n_a_m_e and _p_2_‐_n_a_m_e are the names  assigned  to  the  two
       connected end points.

       Packets  transmitted on one device in the pair are immediately received
       on the other device.  When either device is down, the link state of the
       pair is down.

       vveetthh device pairs are useful for combining the  network  facilities  of
       the  kernel  together  in interesting ways.  A particularly interesting
       use case is to place one end of a vveetthh pair in  one  network  namespace
       and  the other end in another network namespace, thus allowing communi‐
       cation between network namespaces.  To do this,  one  can  provide  the
       nneettnnss parameter when creating the interfaces:

           # ip link add <p1-name> netns <p1-ns> type veth peer <p2-name> netns <p2-ns>

       or, for an existing vveetthh pair, move one side to the other namespace:

           # ip link set <p2-name> netns <p2-ns>

       eetthhttooooll(8)  can  be  used to find the peer of a vveetthh network interface,
       using commands something like:

           # iipp lliinnkk aadddd vvee__AA ttyyppee vveetthh ppeeeerr nnaammee vvee__BB;   # Create veth pair
           # eetthhttooooll --SS vvee__AA;         # Discover interface index of peer
           NIC statistics:
                peer_ifindex: 16
           # iipp lliinnkk || ggrreepp ''^^1166::'';   # Look up interface
           16: ve_B@ve_A: <BROADCAST,MULTICAST,M-DOWN> mtu 1500 qdisc ...

